 "script": [
  {
   "id": "hook",
   "text": "Typhoon Champi dumped over four hundred millimeters on Jeju. What does Japan get next?"
  },
  {
   "id": "f1",
   "text": "South Korea's Jeju Island saw up to four hundred fifteen millimeters of rain over four days, Korean media report. One Mount Halla station logged three hundred forty-one by Sunday evening. Flights and ferries were disrupted, and hiking trails on the mountain were closed."
  },
  {
   "id": "f2",
   "text": "Early Monday, Champi weakened into an extratropical cyclone south of Jeju. Japan's weather agency says it could approach Kyushu. Reports say Tsushima Island logged more than two hundred millimeters in one day, an October record."
  },
  {
   "id": "f3",
   "text": "The agency expects up to two hundred millimeters more in northern Kyushu and Shikoku within twenty-four hours, and one hundred fifty in southern Kyushu. A stalled front is adding to the rain, it says. It warns of landslides and flooding."
  },
  {
   "id": "cta",
   "text": "Will Champi's rain cause flooding in Japan? Yes or no?"
  }
 ],
